<HelpLayout title="Gyms & Equipment" description="How to manage gyms, machines, attachments, bars, accessories, dumbbell racks, and grip types in Badger.">
  <h1>Gyms & Equipment</h1>

  <p>Badger lets you track which gym, machine, and attachment you used for each exercise. This unlocks machine-scoped progress tracking - your cable row on the low pulley and your cable row on the high cable are treated as separate performance histories.</p>

  <hr />

  <h2>Managing gyms</h2>
  <p>Go to <strong>You → Gyms</strong> to manage your gym list.</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Tap <strong>+</strong> to create a gym.</li>
    <li>Tap a gym to edit its name or notes, or to set it as the default.</li>
    <li>The <strong>default gym</strong> is automatically used when starting a workout if you have only one gym, or if a default is set among multiple.</li>
    <li>Each gym has its own <strong>Plate inventory</strong> - open a gym and tap <em>Plate assortment</em> to set which plates (and how many) are available there. Competition gyms often stock both kg and lbs plates, so the set is kept per unit. New gyms start from your global default set, so the plate calculator works out of the box; per-gym editing is optional.</li>
    <li>Gyms can be <strong>archived</strong> (hidden from pickers but preserved for historical data) instead of deleted.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="machines">Machines</h2>
  <p>Go to <strong>You → Machines</strong> to manage machines.</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Tap <strong>+</strong> to create a machine (e.g. "Cable station", "Leg press", "Smith machine"). Give it a name and pick which gyms it lives at - if you only have one gym, it is linked there automatically and Badger doesn't ask. You land on the new machine's page straight away, so you can set the rest of its details while you're still standing in front of it.</li>
    <li>Each machine has two toggles: <strong>Uses attachments</strong> and <strong>Uses grip types</strong>.</li>
    <li>Set the machine's <strong>Resistance type</strong> - <em>Selectorized</em> (a pin-selected weight stack), <em>Plate-loaded</em>, or <em>Unspecified</em> if you'd rather not say. This is the setting that matters most: it decides what the rest of the machine's weight settings even ask you.</li>
    <li>The <strong>Plate calculator</strong> section follows the resistance type, so you're only ever asked something that makes sense for the machine in front of you:
      <ul>
        <li><em>Selectorized</em> - there is no plate calculator section at all. A pin-selected stack has no plates to load, so there is nothing to configure. The weight increment settings below stay, since those are exactly what a stack does need.</li>
        <li><em>Plate-loaded</em> - you choose <em>Dual-Loaded</em> or <em>Single-Loaded</em>. It starts on Dual-Loaded, which is what a machine that has never been configured already behaved as. Dual-Loaded also lets you enter the machine's <strong>Starting weight</strong> (see below). Turning the calculator off isn't offered here: a machine you load with plates has plate math by definition.</li>
        <li><em>Unspecified</em> - you choose <em>None</em>, <em>Dual-Loaded</em> or <em>Single-Loaded</em>, and nothing is selected until you pick one. Until then Badger says so ("Not set") and the exercise's own plate calculator setting applies. <em>None</em> turns the calculator off for this machine entirely.</li>
      </ul>
    </li>
    <li>The <strong>Starting weight</strong> is what the machine weighs empty - the bar, the sled, or the arms, before any plates. It used to be called "Bar weight", which only named half of what it is: plenty of machines have no bar at all. Most aren't labeled either, so it is completely fine to leave it blank - the plate calculator then just counts plates. Tap the <strong>(i)</strong> beside the label for the same explanation in the app.</li>
    <li>On a plate-loaded machine, <strong>How weight is logged</strong> says what a number you type on this machine <em>means</em>: the <strong>Total</strong>, or what is on <strong>one side</strong>. Iso-lateral machines are the reason it exists - if you load 90&nbsp;lbs a side and write down 90, Badger should know that is one side and not quietly file it as 90 total. It applies wherever the machine is used: the weight field says "/ side", the plate calculator reads your number the same way, and every set you log on it is recorded with the answer. Nothing is doubled or converted - your numbers are still exactly the numbers you wrote - and sets you logged before this existed are left alone rather than guessed at. It is only asked for plate-loaded machines that load both sides - a pin-selected stack has one number and no sides, and on a single lever the one side already is the total.</li>
    <li>Set a <strong>Logging unit</strong> if this machine should be logged in a specific unit (kg or lbs) regardless of your app setting - useful for a stack that is physically kg but dual-marked with awkward lbs numbers. Leave it on <em>Default</em> to follow the exercise, then the app setting. When you start a set on the machine, the weight field switches to its unit automatically - and so do the routine editor, quick edit, and the log preview for any routine slot that has this machine as its default, so you plan in the unit you lift in.</li>
    <li>Optionally set a <strong>Weight increment</strong> - the physical step between settings (for example 5&nbsp;kg between stack positions), entered in the machine's own unit. For stacks that change step size partway up, also fill in <strong>Then step</strong> and <strong>Above</strong> (for example 10&nbsp;kg up to 100, then 20&nbsp;kg). The progressive-overload suggestion snaps to this grid, and so do the <strong>+</strong> and <strong>−</strong> buttons beside the weight field while you log - so a stack that steps by 10 moves by 10, not by your exercise's 2.5.</li>
    <li>On the machine's detail screen, use the <strong>Available at</strong> section to link the machine to one or more gyms. A machine that is linked to no gym at all can't be offered while you're training, and the section tells you so.</li>
    <li><strong>Deleting a machine:</strong> if the machine isn't referenced anywhere, deleting it also removes it from every gym and exercise link. If it's used in a logged workout or set as a default in a routine, deletion is blocked so your history and routines stay intact - remove it from those first if you really want it gone.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Linking machines to exercises</h3>
  <p>Linking a machine to an exercise marks the two as compatible, which is what puts the machine at the top of the "Set machine" picker while you train.</p>
  <p>You don't have to set this up in advance. The picker has a second section, <strong>Other machines at this gym</strong>, listing everything else at your current gym. Pick one and Badger links it to the exercise for you, so it's in the top section from then on. Do the linking whenever it suits you: while training, or up front via the exercise library → <strong>Edit</strong> → <strong>Compatible machines</strong>.</p>

  <h2>Attachments</h2>
  <p>Attachments are cable/pulley accessories (e.g. "Straight bar", "Rope", "V-bar", "D-handle"). Go to <strong>You → Attachments</strong> to manage them.</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Tap <strong>+</strong> to create an attachment. Like machines, you name it and pick its gyms (skipped when you only have one), then land on its page to finish up.</li>
    <li>Link attachments to gyms via the <strong>Available at</strong> section on each attachment's detail screen. An attachment with no gym isn't offered while training, and the section says so.</li>
    <li><strong>Deleting an attachment</strong> works like deleting a machine: blocked if it appears in a logged workout or a routine default, otherwise removed from all gyms.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Attachments are available in the machine picker only when the current machine has <strong>Uses attachments</strong> enabled.</p>
  <p>Notes you write on an attachment show up in the training screen's notes card whenever that attachment is selected, next to the machine's own setup notes.</p>

  <h2 id="bars">Bars</h2>
  <p>A bar is a thing you own, not a number on a settings screen. The Olympic bar, the women's bar, the trap bar, the EZ curl, and the worn 45 in the corner that actually weighs 44 are all different bars, so Badger lets you name them and pick one per exercise. Go to <strong>You → Bars</strong> to manage them.</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Tap <strong>+</strong> to create a bar. Like machines and attachments, you name it and pick its gyms, then land on its page to finish up.</li>
    <li>Give it a <strong>weight</strong> in kg or lbs. It is stored exactly as you type it, never converted, so a 45 lb bar stays 45. Leave it blank if you don't know - a bar with no weight is still perfectly useful for the rest of what bars do.</li>
    <li>A bar can carry <strong>notes</strong> and a <a href="#photos">photo</a>, same as a machine.</li>
    <li>Link bars to gyms from the bar's <strong>Available at</strong> section, or from <strong>Bars at this gym</strong> on the gym's own page.</li>
    <li><strong>Deleting a bar</strong> works like deleting a machine: blocked if it appears in a logged workout or as a routine default, otherwise removed from every gym.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3 id="picking-a-bar">Picking a bar while you train</h3>
  <p>On a barbell exercise, at a gym that has bars, a <strong>bar chip</strong> joins the context row under the input fields. Tap it, or use <strong>⋮ → Set bar…</strong>, and pick from the bars at your gym. The picker shows each bar's weight in the unit you're logging in, and has a <strong>New bar…</strong> row at the bottom if you're standing in front of one you never added - it only asks for a name, and you can weigh it later on its own page.</p>
  <p>If the exercise isn't a barbell movement, or you have no gym set, or your gym has no bars linked, nothing bar-related appears. Nobody gets asked which bar they used on a leg press.</p>

  <h3 id="bar-weight">What the bar's weight is used for</h3>
  <p>The bar you pick is the first place Badger looks for a starting weight, ahead of the machine's and then the exercise's own. That one answer feeds both the <a href="/help/tools">plate calculator</a> and the <a href="/help/logging#set-types">warm-up calculator</a>, so they can't disagree with each other.</p>
  <p>A bar with no weight entered doesn't blank anything out - it just falls through, and whatever the machine or exercise already said still applies. Adding "Trap bar" purely so your records stay separate never costs you a bar weight you'd already set up.</p>

  <h3 id="bar-prs">Records are kept per bar</h3>
  <p>A different bar is a different lift, so personal records are tracked per machine <em>and</em> per bar. A deadlift off the trap bar doesn't compete with your straight-bar deadlift, and neither one hides the other.</p>
  <p>Sets logged with no bar recorded - which is every set you logged before bars existed - still see your whole history, exactly as before. Nothing you already logged changed when this arrived.</p>
  <p>A routine slot can carry a <strong>Default bar</strong>, so the right bar is already picked when you start the session. The bar you used shows in your history and on the workout summary, and you can change it after the fact by tapping the equipment line there.</p>

  <h2 id="accessories">Accessories: bands, chains, and straps</h2>
  <p>Accessories are the things you add to a lift that aren't the load itself: resistance bands, chains, suspension straps. Go to <strong>You → Accessories</strong> to manage them. Each one has:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>A <strong>name</strong> and a <strong>kind</strong> - Band, Chain, Suspension (TRX), or Other.</li>
    <li>A <strong>color</strong>. This is the point of the whole screen: people say "the green band", not "the 1.9 cm band". Pick from the preset swatches or set your own. An accessory with no color gets a hollow ring rather than a made-up one.</li>
    <li>An optional <strong>label</strong>, free text, shown beside the name - "rated 25 lbs", "doubled", whatever helps. Badger never reads a number out of it. See below.</li>
    <li><strong>Notes</strong>, and the usual <strong>Available at</strong> gym links.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>There is no photo field, on purpose. A photo of a band tells you nothing the color doesn't.</p>

  <h3 id="picking-accessories">Picking accessories while you train</h3>
  <p>An <strong>accessories chip</strong> joins the context row on a barbell or bodyweight exercise, at a gym that has accessories linked. On a machine it appears only when that machine has <strong>Supports bands</strong> switched on, and only bands are offered there.</p>
  <p>Unlike the machine and bar pickers, this one takes several at once and stays open until you tap Done - two bands stack to a resistance neither one gives you alone, and chains often ride on a bar that's already banded. Unchecking everything and confirming clears the lot; backing out changes nothing.</p>
  <p>The chip then reads back as a count with the colors beside it - <strong>"2 bands"</strong>, <strong>"1 chain"</strong>, or <strong>"3 accessories"</strong> when you've mixed kinds - and the same count and dots show in your history and on the workout summary. Three band names don't fit on a chip; the dots are what tell them apart.</p>

  <h3 id="accessories-and-records">Accessories never touch the math</h3>
  <p>Badger records what you attached and nothing more. No weight is adjusted, no plate math changes, the <strong>+/−</strong> stepper is unaffected, and the label is never parsed. A band's rated tension is not what it contributes at the top of a rep, and Badger would rather tell you nothing than tell you a number it can't stand behind.</p>
  <p>That is also why <strong>sets logged with an accessory attached stay out of your personal records entirely</strong>, in both directions. A banded set never earns a PR badge, and it never blocks one either: a banded 100 kg bench can't stop a bare 95 kg bench from being a record. Clear the accessories off an exercise and its sets go back into the running, so fixing a mistake is just fixing it.</p>
  <p>Two ways to handle a session that's part banded and part bare:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Bands for some sets, then bare for the rest</strong> - add the exercise twice, once with the accessory and once without. The bare rows keep their records.</li>
    <li><strong>Alternating within a session</strong> - do the same thing and put the two in a <a href="/help/logging#supersets">superset</a>, which is how you alternate between anything else.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="racks">Dumbbell and kettlebell racks</h2>
  <p>A weight increment describes a regular ladder. A real rack doesn't run like one: it goes 5, 10, 15 up to 50, then skips to 60 and 70, and stops. So instead of a step size, Badger lets you list what's actually on the rack, and the <strong>+/−</strong> buttons beside the weight field walk that list.</p>
  <p><strong>The rack never restricts anything.</strong> You can still type any weight you like, nothing snaps on save, and no set is ever checked against it. It decides one thing only: which number the stepper lands on next.</p>

  <h3 id="setting-up-a-rack">Setting one up</h3>
  <p>Open a gym and use the <strong>Racks</strong> section - one row for dumbbells, one for kettlebells. Or wait until it's in your way: on a dumbbell or kettlebell exercise at a gym with no rack set up, a quiet <strong>"Set up My Gym's dumbbell rack"</strong> link sits under the weight field. It goes away by itself once a rack exists.</p>
  <ul>
    <li>The first time you open a rack it fills in a sensible starting list and saves it, so tapping the link once already leaves you with a working rack.</li>
    <li>Add weights one at a time, or use <strong>Add range</strong> for a run of them - from, to, and a step. An adjustable dumbbell is just a range.</li>
    <li>The list is the whole story: nothing distinguishes a weight the preset filled in from one you typed, so edit and delete freely.</li>
    <li>kg and lbs racks are kept separately and never converted between each other. A rack is a physical fact about a gym, and 20 kg bells are not 45 lb bells. A <strong>kg / lbs switch</strong> at the top of the editor flips between the two lists, so you can set both up from the same place.</li>
    <li>A rack with fewer than two weights isn't a rack, so the stepper falls back to your ordinary increment until there are at least two.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3 id="rack-colors">Kettlebell colors</h3>
  <p>Competition kettlebells are color-coded by weight, and the kg preset comes with that code already set: 8 pink, 12 blue, 16 yellow, 20 purple, 24 green, 28 orange, 32 red. (4 kg has no standard color, so it starts blank.) Tap the dot beside any weight to change it, or to give one to a dumbbell entry - the color is available on both, it's just far more useful on bells.</p>
  <p>The lbs kettlebell preset is a plain uncolored ladder. The color code marks kilogram bells, and inventing a pound version of it would be making something up.</p>
  <p>While you're logging, a matching dot appears next to the weight whenever it lands exactly on a colored rack weight - a glance-check that you picked up the bell you meant to.</p>

  <h3 id="rack-stepper">What the stepper does with it</h3>
  <p>Press <strong>+</strong> or <strong>−</strong> and the weight moves to the next entry on the rack in that direction. A weight that isn't on the rack moves to its nearest neighbor on that side. Above the top of the rack, or below the bottom, the stepper goes back to using your ordinary increment rather than trapping you inside the list.</p>
  <p>A small line under the field says where the step is coming from: <strong>"Step: rack · My Gym"</strong>. It names the rack rather than a number, because walking a rack has no single step size - the next tap would contradict it.</p>
  <p><strong>Kettlebells in kg at a gym that works in pounds?</strong> Switch the rack editor to kg and set that rack up, then set the exercise's weight unit to kg (exercise library → Edit → Weight unit). Racks follow the unit the exercise is logged in, not your app-wide setting, so both halves matter: the kg rack has to exist, and the exercise has to log in kg for the stepper to walk it. The editor says the same thing right where you'd hit this.</p>

  <h2 id="photos">Photos</h2>
  <p>A machine, attachment, or bar can carry a photo, so you can recognize the right piece of kit when you're standing in front of the rack. Open it and tap the photo panel at the top to <strong>take a photo</strong> or <strong>choose one from your gallery</strong>. Tap it again to replace or remove it. (<a href="#accessories">Accessories</a> don't have one - their color does that job.)</p>
  <p>Attachment photos also show as a small thumbnail in the attachment picker during a workout, which is the point: "the rope or the short V-bar?" is easier to answer from a picture than a name.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Photos stay on your device unless you ask otherwise.</strong> Nothing is uploaded on its own, and Badger only asks for camera or photo access at the moment you first use it.</li>
    <li><strong>Cloud backup leaves photos out unless you turn them on.</strong> Backups stay small and quick by default, so photos are not included and a restore onto a new phone brings your machines and attachments back without their pictures. To carry them across, turn on <strong>Back up equipment photos</strong> on the <a href={`/help/settings#cloud-backup`}>Cloud backup</a> screen. It is off by default, because photos make backups considerably larger and they use your own Drive storage.</li>
    <li>Deleting a machine, attachment, or bar deletes its photo too.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="grip-types">Grip types</h2>
  <p>When a machine has <strong>Uses grip types</strong> enabled, a row of grip chips appears in the training screen for that exercise. Select your grip before or after logging sets:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Overhand</li>
    <li>Underhand</li>
    <li>Neutral</li>
    <li>Wide</li>
    <li>Close</li>
    <li>Hammer</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Tap the active chip again to clear the selection. The grip is stored on the workout exercise record and shown in the history tab.</p>
  <p>You can also set a <strong>default grip type</strong> per routine exercise in the predefined sets screen - this gets pre-filled when starting a routine workout.</p>

  <h2 id="workout-context">Gym context during workouts</h2>

  <h3>Automatic gym assignment</h3>
  <p>When a workout is created, Badger tries to set the gym automatically:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>If you have only one gym → that gym is used.</li>
    <li>If you have a default gym → that gym is used.</li>
    <li>If you have multiple gyms with no default → no gym is set (you can assign it manually).</li>
  </ul>
  <p>If a routine day has a <strong>default gym</strong> configured, that overrides the above when starting from a routine.</p>

  <h3>Changing the gym mid-workout</h3>
  <p>The current gym name appears in the training screen's header bar. To change it: tap <strong>⋮ → Change gym…</strong> and select a different gym.</p>

  <h3>Setting a machine for an exercise</h3>
  <p>Tap <strong>⋮ → Set machine…</strong> to pick a machine and (if applicable) an attachment. The top of the picker lists machines already marked compatible with this exercise at your current gym. Below that, <strong>Other machines at this gym</strong> lists the rest, so nothing at the gym is out of reach - and picking from it marks that machine compatible for next time.</p>
  <p>Badger remembers the last attachment used for each exercise + machine combination and pre-selects it next time.</p>

  <h2>Progress filtering by machine</h2>
  <p>On the Progress screen, if you've used the same exercise on multiple machines, a row of filter chips appears. Select a specific machine to see only that machine's history, or select <strong>All</strong> to see combined data.</p>
  <p>The most recently used machine is selected by default. The same filter applies to PRs - they're scoped per machine when a machine is set, and per <a href="#bars">bar</a> when a bar is. The filter chips themselves are machine-only for now: the bar you used is named on every row, but you can't yet narrow the list to it.</p>

  <h2>Routine defaults</h2>
  <p>You can set default equipment at the routine level to avoid re-selecting it each session:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Routine day → Default gym</strong> - Pre-sets the workout gym when starting from this day.</li>
    <li><strong>Routine exercise → Default machine & attachment</strong> - Pre-sets the machine for that exercise when starting from a routine.</li>
    <li><strong>Routine exercise → Default bar</strong> - Pre-sets the <a href="#bars">bar</a> on a barbell exercise.</li>
    <li><strong>Routine exercise → Default grip type</strong> - Pre-sets the grip for that exercise.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Accessories are picked per session rather than planned, so there is no routine default for them.</p>
</HelpLayout>
